  "problem": "A particular shop sells watermelons and melons. The weight of a watermelon is normally distributed with a mean of $10\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{kg}.$ The weight of a melon is normally distributed with a mean of $5\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{kg}.$ If one watermelon and one melon are randomly selected, what is the probability that the weight of the watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of the melon? You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ | $\\,0.7224\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ | $\\,0.7549\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ | $\\,0.7852\\,$ |",
